Neural networks learn from examples instead of relying on manually programmed rules, which makes them useful for tasks such as handwritten-digit recognition.
Perceptrons are simple artificial neurons that combine inputs using weights and a bias to produce a binary output of 0 or 1.
Sigmoid neurons improve on perceptrons by producing smooth outputs between 0 and 1, allowing small parameter changes to create small output changes.
A neural network typically contains an input layer, one or more hidden layers, and an output layer. Hidden layers learn useful intermediate patterns or features.
For handwritten-digit recognition, the network uses 784 input neurons for the \(28 \times 28\) image pixels and 10 output neurons, one for each digit from 0 to 9.
The network is trained using the MNIST dataset, which contains 60,000 training images and 10,000 test images of handwritten digits.
A cost function measures how far the network's prediction is from the correct answer. Training aims to adjust weights and biases so that this cost becomes as small as possible.
Gradient descent reduces the cost by repeatedly changing weights and biases in the direction opposite to the gradient. The learning rate controls how large each update is.
Stochastic gradient descent (SGD) speeds up training by using small groups of examples called mini-batches instead of the entire dataset at once. One complete pass through the training data is called an epoch.